===Architecture===
In the 1970s, botanists [[Francis Hallé|Hallé]], Oldeman and [[P. B. Tomlinson|Tomlinson]] studied the growth patterns of tropical trees and described a set of 23 "architectural models", named after various botanists, which can be seen as different growth strategy to occupy space.<ref name="TTF">{{Cite book |last1=Hallé |first1=Francis |title=Tropical trees and forests: an architectural analysis |last2=Oldeman |first2=Roelof A. A. |last3=Tomlinson |first3=P. B. |last4=Tomlinson |first4=P. B. |date=1978 |publisher=Springer |isbn=978-3-540-08494-5 |location=Berlin Heidelberg}}</ref> ''Araucaria bidwillii'', like many species from the [[Araucariaceae]] family and the [[Abies|fir]] genus, changes its structural model over time. Initially its follows ''[[Jean Massart|Massart]]'s model'' (a single, upright trunk that grows steadily and produces neat, horizontal layers of branches at regular intervals)<ref name="TTF"/>{{rp|p=191}} and gradually changes to ''[[Werner Rauh|Rauh]]'s model'' (like Massart's, but here each branch grows structurally similar to the trunk, with smaller branches branching off of it.)<ref name="TTF"/>{{rp|p=221}}

===Phenology===
The trees' pollen cones appear in April and mature in September or October.<ref>The Gymnosperm Database https://www.conifers.org/ar/Araucaria_bidwillii.php</ref> The cones require fifteen months to mature,<ref>{{cite book | last1= Allen | first1= Richard | last2= Baker | first2= Kimbal | date= 2009 | title= Australia's Remarkable Trees | location= Melbourne | publisher= Miegunyah Press| pages= 23–27}}</ref> and the cones fall 17 to 18 months after pollination in late January to early March from the coast to the current [[Bunya Mountains]]. When there is heavy rainfall or drought, pollination may vary.<ref>Australian Journal of Botany 70(8) p 515 https://doi.org/10.1071/BT22043
2022</ref>

==Taxonomy==
The species was described by the English botanist [[William Jackson Hooker]] in 1843, based on material collected in the "Mount Brisbane range of hills, 70 miles N.W. of Moreton Bay" by [[John Carne Bidwill]] in 1842.{{R|APNI|HOOKER}}{{rp|498}} Hooker states in his paper that Bidwill took "not only branches and cones and male flowers, but also a healthy young living plant" to England where Hooker set about describing the new species, and his paper (titled "Figure and description of a new species of Araucaria from Moreton Bay, New Holland") was published in his own journal ''London Journal of Botany''.{{R|HOOKER}}{{rp|498}}

===Etymology===
The [[genus]] name ''Araucaria'' is taken from the Spanish word {{Lang|es|Araucanía}}, the name of the area in Chile where the first species of this genus originated, and/or {{Lang|es|Araucanos}}, the Spanish word for the original inhabitants of the area.{{R|COOPER2|ANSPA}} Hooker coined the [[Botanical name#Binary name|species epithet]] in honour of Bidwill, for his efforts in collecting specimens and bringing them to him.{{R|HOOKER}}{{rp|502}}

===Vernacular names===
In various [[Australian Aboriginal languages]], this tree is known as ''banya'' (anglicised as ''bunya''), ''bonye, bon-yi''<ref name="CCPETRIE">{{cite book |last1=Petrie |first1=Constance Campbell |title=Tom Petrie's Reminiscences of Early Queensland |date=1837 |publisher=Watson Ferguson & Co. |location=Brisbane |url=https://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks20/2000451h.html |chapter=Chapter II}}</ref> (in [[Gubbi Gubbi language|Gubbi Gubbi]]), ''bunyi'' or ''bunya-bunya'', leading to its common name 'bunya pine'.<ref>Queensland Government Species profile — Araucaria bidwillii (bunya pine)https://apps.des.qld.gov.au/species-search/details/?id=13706</ref> It is also less-known as the false monkey puzzle tree (not to be confused with ''[[Araucaria araucana]]'', the monkey puzzle tree).<ref>{{cite web | title=False monkey puzzle | website=Stuff | date=25 August 2009 | url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/waikato-times/life-style/gardening/548709/False-monkey-puzzle | access-date=3 December 2020|others=From [[Waikato Times]].}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | title=Araucaria bidwillii Bunya-Bunya, Monkey Puzzle Tree, False Monkey Puzzle PFAF Plant Database | website=Plants for a future| url=https://pfaf.org/user/Plant.aspx?LatinName=Araucaria+bidwillii | access-date=3 December 2020}}</ref>

==Evolution==
The bunya pine is a member of the Section ''Bunya'' of the genus ''Araucaria'', and is the sole extant species within it.{{R|TGD|ACS}} This section is thought to have been most widespread in the [[Mesozoic]] – fossils from the [[Jurassic]] period with cone morphology similar to ''A.&nbsp;bidwillii'' have been found in the UK (''[[Araucaria sphaerocarpa]]'') and South America (''[[Araucaria mirabilis]]''){{R|HERNANDEZ}}

==Distribution==
At the start of British occupation, ''A.&nbsp;bidwillii'' was abundant in southern Queensland, occurring in large groves or sprinkled regularly as an [[Rainforest#Emergent layer|emergent]] species throughout other forest types on the upper [[Stanley River (Queensland)|Stanley]] and [[Brisbane River]]s, Sunshine Coast hinterland (especially the [[Blackall Range]] and [[Maleny, Queensland|Maleny]]), and also towards and on the Bunya Mountains.

Two more natural, but very small and very isolated, populations of the species occur approximately {{convert|1500|km|mi|abbr=on}} to the north, in the wet tropics region of northeastern Queensland – one close to [[Cannabullen Falls]] on the [[Atherton Tableland]], and the other in the [[Mount Lewis National Park]].{{R|COL|RFK}}

Today, the southeast Queensland populations exist as very small groves or single trees in its former range, except on and near the Bunya Mountains, where it is still fairly prolific, while the populations in north Queensland remain stable.<ref>{{Cite web |date=4 May 2024 |title=Araucaria bidwillii |url=https://anpsa.org.au/plant_profiles/araucaria-bidwillii/ |access-date=4 May 2024 |website=Australian Native Plants Society (Australia)}}</ref>

The limited distribution of ''A.&nbsp;bidwillii'' in Australia is in part due to poor seed dispersal, and also the drying out of the Australian continent over the millennia, leading to a reduction of areas with suitable climatic zones for rainforest.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Falster |first1=Georgina M. |last2=Wright |first2=Nicky M. |last3=Abram |first3=Nerilie J. |last4=Ukkola |first4=Anna M. |last5=Henley |first5=Benjamin J. |date=27 March 2024 |title=Potential for historically unprecedented Australian droughts from natural variability and climate change |url=https://hess.copernicus.org/articles/28/1383/2024/ |journal=Hydrology and Earth System Sciences |volume=28 |issue=6 |pages=1383–1401 |doi=10.5194/hess-28-1383-2024 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2024HESS...28.1383F |via=European Geosciences Union}}</ref>

==Ecology==
A variety of birds and animals, including [[sulphur-crested cockatoo]]s (''Cacatua galerita''), [[short-eared possum]]s (''Trichosurus caninus''), [[Fawn-footed mosaic-tailed rat|fawn-footed melomys]] (''Melomys cervinipes''), and [[Wallaby|wallabies]] are known to eat the seeds.{{R|BMNP|ABCNEWS|TELLA}} The cockatoos are also a dispersal agent as they will carry seeds to a distant perch to eat, but may drop them on the way.{{R|TELLA}}

The suggestion that extinct large animals (initially dinosaurs and later the [[Australian megafauna]]) may have been dispersers for the bunya is reasonable, given the size of the seeds and their energy content, but difficult to confirm given the incompleteness of the fossil record for [[coprolites]].{{citation needed|date=April 2023}}

''A. bidwillii'' has an unusual cryptogeal seed germination in which the seeds develop to form an underground tuber from which the aerial shoot later emerges. The actual emergence of the seed is then known to occur over several years presumably as a strategy to allow the seedlings to emerge under optimum climatic conditions or, it has been suggested, to avoid fire. This erratic germination has been one of the main problems in [[silviculture]] of the species.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Smith |first1=Ian R. |last2=Butler |first2=Don |date=November 2002 |title=The Bunya in Queensland's Forests |url=https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/queensland-review/article/abs/bunya-in-queenslands-forests/A452919748724D11AF8F75064F9807C6 |journal=Queensland Review |language=en |volume=9 |issue=2 |pages=31–38 |doi=10.1017/S1321816600002932 |issn=1321-8166|url-access=subscription }}</ref>

A problem in small forestry plantations of the bunya pine in Southeast Queensland is the introduction of red deer (''[[Cervus elaphus]]''). Unlike possums and rodents, the deer eat bunya cones while still intact, preventing their dispersal.<ref>State of Queensland, 2013, Feral deer management
strategy 2013–18, p. 7</ref>

==Cultural significance==
The ''bunya'', ''bonye'', ''bunyi'', ''bunya-bunya'' or ''banya'' tree produces edible kernels. The ripe cones fall to the ground. Each segment contains a kernel in a tough protective shell, which will split when boiled or put in a fire. The flavour of the kernel is often compared to a chestnut, although it is less intense in terms of aroma and flavour. The savory flavour and aroma is also comparable to cooked potato.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Moura Nadolny |first1=Jaqueline |last2=Best |first2=Odette |last3=Hassall |first3=Emma |last4=Shewan |first4=Heather M. |last5=Olarte Mantilla |first5=Sandra M. |last6=Stokes |first6=Jason R. |last7=Smyth |first7=Heather E. |date=2022-05-20 |title=Sensory properties of Australian bunya nuts |journal=Journal of Food Science |volume=87 |issue=6 |language=en |pages=1750–3841.16184 |doi=10.1111/1750-3841.16184 |pmid=35593256 |pmc=9325081 |issn=0022-1147}}</ref>

The cones were a very important food source for native Australians – each Aboriginal family would own a group of trees and these would be passed down from generation to generation.{{R|PFAF}} This is said to be the only case of hereditary personal property owned by the Aboriginal people.{{citation needed|date=April 2023}}

After the cones had fallen and the fruit was ripe, a large festival harvest would sometimes occur, between two and seven years apart. The people of the region would set aside differences and gather in the Bon-yi Mountains ([[Bunya Mountains]]) to feast on the kernels. The local people, who were bound by custodial obligations and rights, sent out messengers to invite people from hundreds of kilometres to meet at specific sites. The meetings involved [[Aboriginal ceremonies]], dispute settlements and fights, marriage arrangements and the trading of goods.{{R|HAEBICH}}

In what was probably Australia's largest Indigenous event,{{Citation needed|date=May 2024|reason=This sounds like a personal opinion - please provide a reference}} diverse tribes – up to thousands of people – once travelled great distances (from as far as [[Charleville, Queensland|Charleville]], [[Bundaberg]], [[Dubbo]] and [[Grafton, New South Wales|Grafton]]) to the gatherings. They stayed for months, to celebrate and feast on the bunya nut. The bunya gatherings were an armistice accompanied by much trade exchange, and discussions and negotiations over marriage and regional issues. Due to the sacred status of the bunyas, some tribes would not camp amongst these trees. Also in some regions, the tree was never to be cut.<ref name="Tindale">{{cite book |last=Tindale |first=Norman B. |title=Aboriginal Tribes of Australia: Their Terrain, Environmental Controls, Distribution, Limits, and Proper Names |publisher=Australian National University Press |year=1974 |isbn=0-7081-0741-9 |location=Canberra |pages=80, 124–6}}</ref><ref>Jerome, P., 2002. Boobarran Ngummin: the Bunya Mountains. [Opening address to the Bunya Symposium (2002: Griffith University).].</ref>

Representatives of many different groups from across southern Queensland and northern [[New South Wales]] would meet to discuss important issues relating to the environment, social relationships, politics and The Dreaming lore, feasting and sharing dance ceremonies. Many conflicts would be settled at this event, and consequences for breaches of laws were discussed.<ref name=resource8>{{cite report|url=https://www.qcaa.qld.edu.au/downloads/approach2/indigenous_res010_0802.pdf|publisher=Queensland Government and Queensland Studies Authority|series=Resource: Indigenous Perspectives: Res008|date=February 2008|title=Aboriginal Ceremonies|access-date=17 January 2020}}</ref>

A Bunya festival was recorded by [[Thomas Petrie]] (1831–1910), who went with the Aboriginal people of Brisbane at the age of 14 to the festival at the Bunya Range (now the Blackall Range in the hinterland area of the Sunshine Coast). His daughter, Constance Petrie, put down his stories in which he said that the trees fruited at three-year intervals.<ref>{{SLQ-CC-BY|url=https://www.slq.qld.gov.au/blog/bountiful-bunyas-charismatic-tree-fascinating-history|title=Bountiful bunyas : a charismatic tree with a fascinating history|date=11 October 2021|author(s)=Stacey Larner|accessdate=31 May 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://anpsa.org.au/APOL8/dec97-3.html|title=Bunya Feast|access-date=16 February 2012|website=anpsa.org.au}}</ref> The three-year interval may not be correct. [[Ludwig Leichhardt]] wrote in 1844 of his expedition to the Bunya feast.<ref>{{cite journal|title=Ludwig Leichhardt (on the bushfood trail) |journal=Australian Bushfoods Magazine |year=1997 |issue=1 |url=http://www.ausbushfoods.com/backissues/Issue1/issue_1_13.htm |access-date=30 October 2012 |issn=1447-0489 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131105202527/http://www.ausbushfoods.com/backissues/Issue1/issue_1_13.htm |archive-date=5 November 2013 }}</ref>

In 1842, the government of what was then the [[Colony of New South Wales]] published a notice in the [[Government Gazette of the State of New South Wales|N.S.W. Government Gazette]] which prohibited settlers from occupying land or cutting timber within a proclaimed "Bunya district". This may have been in recognition of the local Aboriginal people's close association with these trees,{{R|Heap1965}} or their "fierce protection" of them.{{R|HAEBICH}} Regardless, the proclamation was repealed in 1860 in one of the first acts of the government of the newly created [[Colony of Queensland]].<ref name="Heap1965"/> The Aboriginal people were eventually driven out of the forests and the festivals ceased.{{R|HAEBICH}} The forests were felled for timber and cleared to make way for cultivation.{{R|HAEBICH}}

===Today===
Indigenous groups such as the [[Wakka Wakka|Wakawaka]], [[Githabul]], [[Kabi Kabi]], [[Jarowair]], [[Goreng goreng]], [[Butchulla]], [[Quandamooka]], [[Baruŋgam]], [[Yiman people|Yiman]] and [[Wulili]] have continued cultural and spiritual connections to the Bunya Mountains to this day. A number of strategies including the use of [[traditional ecological knowledge]] have been incorporated into the current management practices of the national park and conservation reserves with the Bunya Murri Ranger project currently operating in the mountains.<ref>Markwell Consulting, 2010. Bonye Buru Booburrgan Ngmmunge – Bunya Mountains Aboriginal Aspirations and Caring for Country Plan (Plan).</ref><ref>Queensland Government, 2012. Bunya Mountains National Park Management Statement 2012 (Management Plan). Department of National Parks, Recreation, Sport and Racing.</ref>

